WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.211
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:08.688
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:17.364
Unit fifty-six: Global Health Ethics. In this unit, you will argue formally about fairness in global health.

4
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.781
triage ethics.

5
00:00:20.481 --> 00:00:25.220
triage ethics means the moral rules for deciding which patients are treated first.

6
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.691
Triage ethics became urgent when hospitals filled during COVID-19.

7
00:00:30.391 --> 00:00:34.042
Your turn. Say: triage ethics.

8
00:00:36.742 --> 00:00:38.149
triage ethics.

9
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.708
resource allocation.

10
00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:46.798
resource allocation means deciding how limited money, staff or medicine is shared.

11
00:00:47.098 --> 00:00:50.189
Fair resource allocation saves the most lives.

12
00:00:50.889 --> 00:00:54.656
Your turn. Say: resource allocation.

13
00:00:57.356 --> 00:00:58.821
resource allocation.

14
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:01.572
global health justice.

15
00:01:02.272 --> 00:01:08.997
global health justice means the principle that everyone deserves fair access to health, wherever they live.

16
00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:13.166
Global health justice demands vaccines for poor countries too.

17
00:01:13.866 --> 00:01:17.807
Your turn. Say: global health justice.

18
00:01:20.507 --> 00:01:22.181
global health justice.

19
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:24.359
patent waiver.

20
00:01:25.059 --> 00:01:31.017
patent waiver means temporary suspension of patent rights so medicines can be produced more widely.

21
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:35.499
Many countries called for a patent waiver on COVID-19 vaccines.

22
00:01:36.199 --> 00:01:39.885
Your turn. Say: patent waiver.

23
00:01:42.585 --> 00:01:43.714
patent waiver.

24
00:01:44.717 --> 00:01:46.298
access to medicines.

25
00:01:46.998 --> 00:01:52.446
access to medicines means people's ability to obtain affordable, safe medicines.

26
00:01:52.746 --> 00:01:56.162
Access to medicines is part of the right to health.

27
00:01:56.862 --> 00:02:00.362
Your turn. Say: access to medicines.

28
00:02:03.062 --> 00:02:04.725
access to medicines.

29
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:07.229
health sovereignty.

30
00:02:07.929 --> 00:02:13.411
health sovereignty means a country's ability to produce and control its own health supplies.

31
00:02:13.711 --> 00:02:17.127
The pandemic made health sovereignty a priority.

32
00:02:17.827 --> 00:02:21.548
Your turn. Say: health sovereignty.

33
00:02:24.248 --> 00:02:25.620
health sovereignty.

34
00:02:26.617 --> 00:02:28.047
pandemic treaty.

35
00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:34.079
pandemic treaty means an international agreement on preventing and responding to pandemics.

36
00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:40.477
The World Health Assembly adopted a pandemic agreement in 2025.

37
00:02:41.177 --> 00:02:44.804
Your turn. Say: pandemic treaty.

38
00:02:47.504 --> 00:02:48.796
pandemic treaty.

39
00:02:49.783 --> 00:02:51.678
medical humanitarianism.

40
00:02:52.378 --> 00:02:58.070
medical humanitarianism means providing health care to people in crisis regardless of politics.

41
00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.134
Medical humanitarianism brings surgeons to war zones.

42
00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:06.497
Your turn. Say: medical humanitarianism.

43
00:03:09.197 --> 00:03:11.022
medical humanitarianism.

44
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:14.167
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:19.276
Rithy, during COVID-19, rich countries bought most of the vaccines first.

46
00:03:20.583 --> 00:03:25.543
Global health justice demands that every country receive a fair share.

47
00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.791
Some proposed a patent waiver.

48
00:03:30.083 --> 00:03:33.766
Were it granted, poorer nations could produce their own vaccines.

49
00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:37.711
Pharmaceutical firms objected strongly.

50
00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:42.340
Be that as it may, lives must come before profits.

51
00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.520
What about triage in overwhelmed hospitals?

52
00:03:47.817 --> 00:03:52.150
It is vital that each decision be guided by clear and fair principles.

53
00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:58.642
Grammar focus: Subjunctive and archaic forms (advanced).

54
00:03:58.942 --> 00:04:02.370
The subjunctive adds formality and moral force.

55
00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:06.748
Global health justice demands that every country receive a fair share.

56
00:04:07.048 --> 00:04:10.475
Be that as it may, lives must come before profits.

57
00:04:10.775 --> 00:04:17.465
'Be that as it may' concedes a point formally; the mandative subjunctive drops the -s even with he, she or it.

58
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.915
Now read and listen. Who Gets Treated First?.

59
00:04:22.215 --> 00:04:28.255
Health is often described as a human right, yet access to medicines remains deeply unequal.

60
00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:36.786
The COVID-19 pandemic exposed this inequality starkly, as wealthy nations secured vaccines first.

61
00:04:38.083 --> 00:04:44.460
Advocates of global health justice demanded that vaccines be shared according to need.

62
00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:52.068
Some countries proposed a patent waiver, while manufacturers argued that patents fund innovation.

63
00:04:53.383 --> 00:04:59.806
The crisis also renewed interest in health sovereignty, as nations sought to produce their own supplies.

64
00:05:01.117 --> 00:05:11.046
In 2025, member states of the World Health Organization adopted a pandemic agreement intended to improve future cooperation.

65
00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:18.727
Inside hospitals, triage ethics and resource allocation forced doctors to make painful choices.

66
00:05:20.017 --> 00:05:26.579
Medical humanitarianism reminds us that, be that as it may, the duty to heal knows no borders.

67
00:05:27.883 --> 00:05:30.501
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:34.379
Temporarily suspending patent rights on medicines is a blank.

69
00:05:34.679 --> 00:05:39.947
A, patent waiver. B, pandemic treaty. C, triage ethics.

70
00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.658
The answer is A: patent waiver.

71
00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.229
It is essential that every child blank vaccinated.

72
00:05:51.529 --> 00:05:54.474
A, be. B, is. C, was.

73
00:05:58.483 --> 00:06:00.018
The answer is A: be.

74
00:06:01.517 --> 00:06:03.423
'Be that as it may' means blank.

75
00:06:03.723 --> 00:06:09.327
A, even if that is true. B, because of that. C, as it should be.

76
00:06:13.317 --> 00:06:15.885
The answer is A: even if that is true.

77
00:06:17.383 --> 00:06:21.275
A country's ability to produce its own health supplies is blank.

78
00:06:21.575 --> 00:06:29.292
A, health sovereignty. B, resource allocation. C, medical humanitarianism.

79
00:06:33.283 --> 00:06:36.699
The answer is A: health sovereignty.

80
00:06:38.183 --> 00:06:42.667
According to the text, what was adopted in 2025?

81
00:06:42.967 --> 00:06:49.291
A, a pandemic agreement. B, a vaccine patent. C, a new health tax.

82
00:06:53.283 --> 00:06:56.200
The answer is A: a pandemic agreement.

83
00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:05.837
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should rich countries share vaccines with poor countries before vaccinating everyone at home?

84
00:07:11.137 --> 00:07:15.401
Part two: Use the mandative subjunctive and 'be that as it may'.

85
00:07:21.701 --> 00:07:34.375
Global justice demands that the most vulnerable be protected first, wherever they live. Some say governments must serve their own citizens. Be that as it may, a virus anywhere is a threat everywhere.

86
00:07:34.675 --> 00:07:37.186
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:38.183 --> 00:07:43.692
Great work today! You learned eight new words and subjunctive and archaic forms (advanced).

88
00:07:43.992 --> 00:07:47.410
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

89
00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.419
See you in Unit fifty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:51.719 --> 00:07:54.116
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

